(window.RW_DISPLAY = window.RW_DISPLAY || {})["test08_1A"] = [{"id":"14b7dced","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"The following text is from Walt Whitman’s 1860 poem “Calamus 24.” I HEAR it is charged against me that I seek to destroy institutions; But really I am neither for nor against institutions (What indeed have I in common with them? —Or what with the destruction of them?), Only I will establish in the Mannahatta [Manhattan] and in every city of These States, inland and seaboard, And in the fields and woods, and above every keel [ship] little or large, that dents the water, Without edifices, or rules, or trustees, or any argument, The institution of the dear love of comrades.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is from Walt Whitman’s 1860 poem “Calamus 24.”","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"I HEAR it is charged against me that I seek to destroy institutions;","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"But really I am neither for nor against institutions","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"(What indeed have I in common with them?—Or what with the destruction of them?),","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Only I will establish in the Mannahatta [Manhattan] and in every city of These States, inland and seaboard,","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"And in the fields and woods, and above every keel [ship] little or large, that dents the water,","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Without edifices, or rules, or trustees, or any argument,","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"The institution of the dear love of comrades.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["The speaker questions an increasingly prevalent attitude, then summarizes his worldview.","The speaker regrets his isolation from others, then predicts a profound change in society.","The speaker concedes his personal shortcomings, then boasts of his many achievements.","The speaker addresses a criticism leveled against him, then announces a grand ambition of his."]},{"id":"4ed09415","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"Vadamalai Elangovan and Ganapathi Marimuthu showed that high moonlight intensity inhibits the activity of the greater short-nosed fruit bat (Cynopterus sphinx), a result explicable in terms of benefits and costs: greater lunar intensity may not enable the bats to increase foraging success enough to offset the higher chance of detection by predatory owls or hawks. Most other nocturnal mammals respond to lunar intensity variations similarly to greater short-nosed fruit bats, but mongoose lemurs (Eulemur mongoz) display the opposite pattern, as their heavy reliance on visual foraging results in a different balance of reward and risk.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Vadamalai Elangovan and Ganapathi Marimuthu showed that high moonlight intensity inhibits the activity of the greater short-nosed fruit bat","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"(Cynopterus sphinx), a result explicable in terms of benefits and costs: greater lunar intensity may not enable the bats to increase foraging","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"success enough to offset the higher chance of detection by predatory owls or hawks. Most other nocturnal mammals respond to lunar intensity","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"variations similarly to greater short-nosed fruit bats, but mongoose lemurs (Eulemur mongoz) display the opposite pattern, as their heavy reliance","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"on visual foraging results in a different balance of reward and risk.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It discusses two different responses to the same natural phenomenon, explains why one of those responses occurs, and then suggests that the other response still needs to be explained.","It describes and accounts for a finding, characterizes the finding as representative of a general pattern, and then describes and accounts for an exception to that pattern.","It presents and explains a study result, indicates that the result is similar to the results of many other studies, and then attributes a conflicting study result to a difference in that study’s methods.","It introduces an observation of a behavioral pattern, presents an explanation for the pattern, and then describes an exception to the pattern that casts doubt on that explanation."]},{"id":"213a25d1","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"The following text is from Paul Laurence Dunbar’s 1913 poem “The Poet and His Song.” A song is but a little thing, And yet what joy it is to sing! In hours of toil it gives me zest, And when at eve I long for rest; When cows come home along the bars, And in the fold I hear the bell, As Night, the shepherd, herds his stars, I sing my song, and all is well.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is from Paul Laurence Dunbar’s 1913 poem “The Poet and His Song.”","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"A song is but a little thing,","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"And yet what joy it is to sing!","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"In hours of toil it gives me zest,","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"And when at eve I long for rest;","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"When cows come home along the bars,","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"And in the fold I hear the bell,","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"As Night, the shepherd, herds his stars,","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"I sing my song, and all is well.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To convey how engaging in song makes the speaker feel","To compare the speaker to a singing shepherd","To portray the speaker’s excitement about farming","To describe the pieces of music the speaker enjoys hearing"]},{"id":"2c82068f","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The following text is from Mark Oshiro’s 2018 novel Anger Is a Gift. In the novel, Moss and his friends are on a subway train in Northern California. Lights from the outside world then filled the train car as it rose out of the ground and climbed the elevated track. As long as Moss had lived in West Oakland, he’d never tired of this specific view, so he pointed toward the windows. “Check it,” he said, and the Port of Oakland began to pass by them. ©2018 by Mark Oshiro As used in the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is from Mark Oshiro’s 2018 novel Anger Is a Gift. In the novel, Moss and his friends are on a subway train in Northern","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"California.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Lights from the outside world then filled the train car as it rose out of the ground and climbed the elevated track. As long as Moss","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"had lived in West Oakland, he’d never tired of this specific view, so he pointed toward the windows. “Check it,” he said, and the Port","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"of Oakland began to pass by them.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"©2018 by Mark Oshiro","indent":4}],"question":"As used in the text, what does the word “specific” most nearly mean?","choices":["Imaginary","Energetic","Correct","Particular"],"underline":"specific","underline_segments":["specific"]},{"id":"aa5897b8","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"In Jane Austen’s novel Mansfield Park, an almost imperceptible smile from potential suitor Henry Crawford causes the protagonist Fanny Price to blush; her embarrassment grows when she suspects that he is aware of it. This moment — in which Fanny not only infers Henry’s mental state through his gestures, but also infers that he is drawing inferences about her mental state —illustrates what literary scholar George Butte calls “deep intersubjectivity,” a technique for representing interactions between consciousnesses through which Austen’s novels derive much of their social and psychological drama.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In Jane Austen’s novel Mansfield Park, an almost imperceptible smile from potential suitor Henry Crawford causes the protagonist Fanny Price to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"blush; her embarrassment grows when she suspects that he is aware of it. This moment—in which Fanny not only infers Henry’s mental state","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"through his gestures, but also infers that he is drawing inferences about her mental state—illustrates what literary scholar George Butte calls","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"“deep intersubjectivity,” a technique for representing interactions between consciousnesses through which Austen’s novels derive much of their","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"social and psychological drama.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It states a claim about Austen’s skill at representing psychological complexity that is reinforced by an example presented in the following sentence.","It advances an interpretation of an Austen protagonist who is contrasted with protagonists from other Austen novels cited in the following sentence.","It describes a recurring theme in Austen’s novels that is the focus of a literary scholar’s analysis summarized in the following sentence.","It provides a synopsis of an interaction in an Austen novel that illustrates a literary concept discussed in the following sentence."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["In Jane Austen’s novel","Mansfield Park,","an almost imperceptible smile from potential suitor Henry Crawford causes the protagonist Fanny Price to","blush; her embarrassment grows when she suspects that he is aware of it."]},{"id":"37219ba2","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The following text is from John Muir’s 1913 autobiography The Story of My Boyhood and Youth. Muir describes being on a boat. The water was so clear that it was almost invisible, and when we floated slowly out over the plants and fishes, we seemed to be miraculously sustained in the air while exploring a veritable fairyland. As used in the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is from John Muir’s 1913 autobiography The Story of My Boyhood and Youth. Muir describes being on a boat.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"The water was so clear that it was almost invisible, and when we floated slowly out over the plants and fishes, we seemed to be","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"miraculously sustained in the air while exploring a veritable fairyland.","indent":2}],"question":"As used in the text, what does the word “clear” most nearly mean?","choices":["Simple","Understandable","Obvious","Transparent"],"underline":"clear","underline_segments":["clear"]},{"id":"9aa44886","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The following text is from F. Scott Fitzgerald’s 1925 novel The Great Gatsby. [Jay Gatsby] was balancing himself on the dashboard of his car with that resourcefulness of movement that is so peculiarly American — that comes, I suppose, with the absence of lifting work in youth and, even more, with the formless grace of our nervous, sporadic games. This quality was continually breaking through his punctilious manner in the shape of restlessness. As used in the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is from F. Scott Fitzgerald’s 1925 novel The Great Gatsby.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"[Jay Gatsby] was balancing himself on the dashboard of his car with that resourcefulness of movement that is so peculiarly","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"American—that comes, I suppose, with the absence of lifting work in youth and, even more, with the formless grace of our","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"nervous, sporadic games. This quality was continually breaking through his punctilious manner in the shape of restlessness.","indent":2}],"question":"As used in the text, what does the word “quality” most nearly mean?","choices":["Standard","Prestige","Characteristic","Accomplishment"],"underline":"quality","underline_segments":["quality"]},{"id":"a7991da1","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"A unique dialect, or regional variety, of Spanish is spoken in Puerto Rico. It contains many words borrowed from the language of the Taínos, the Indigenous people of Puerto Rico. African languages also made important contributions to the Puerto Rican dialect. For example, the way certain vowel sounds are pronounced in it can be ______ to how they are pronounced in Yoruba, a West African language.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"A unique dialect, or regional variety, of Spanish is spoken in Puerto Rico. It contains many words borrowed from the language of the Taínos, the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Indigenous people of Puerto Rico. African languages also made important contributions to the Puerto Rican dialect. For example, the way certain","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"vowel sounds are pronounced in it can be ______ to how they are pronounced in Yoruba, a West African language.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["traced","surrendered","announced","offered"]},{"id":"63e7799d","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"In vertical inheritance, parents pass genes to their offspring, but in horizontal transfer (HT), one species, often bacteria, passes genetic material to an unrelated species. In a 2022 study, herpetologist Atsushi Kurabayashi and his team investigated HT in multicellular organisms — namely, snakes and frogs in Madagascar. The team detected BovB — — a gene transmitted vertically in snakes in many frog species. The apparent direction of gene transfer seems counterintuitive because frogs usually don’t survive encounters with snakes and so wouldn’t be able to transmit the newly acquired gene to offspring, but the team concluded that BovB is indeed transmitted from snakes to frogs, either directly or indirectly, via HT.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In vertical inheritance, parents pass genes to their offspring, but in horizontal transfer (HT), one species, often bacteria, passes genetic material","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"to an unrelated species. In a 2022 study, herpetologist Atsushi Kurabayashi and his team investigated HT in multicellular organisms—namely,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"snakes and frogs in Madagascar. The team detected BovB—a gene transmitted vertically in snakes—in many frog species. The apparent direction","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"of gene transfer seems counterintuitive because frogs usually don’t survive encounters with snakes and so wouldn’t be able to transmit the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"newly acquired gene to offspring, but the team concluded that BovB is indeed transmitted from snakes to frogs, either directly or indirectly, via","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"HT.","indent":0}],"question":"Which finding, if true, would most directly support the team’s conclusion?","choices":["BovB can be transmitted across frog species through HT.","Parasites known to feed on species of snakes and frogs in which the BovB gene occurs also carry BovB.","BovB cannot be reliably transmitted from a snake species to bacteria that are usually encountered by frog species.","Frog species with BovB show few discernible advantages as compared with frog species that do not carry BovB."]},{"id":"ff18829b","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Average Ratings of Urban Agriculture’s Contribution to Various Social or Ecological Services 5 Rating (0 = no contribution, 5 = high contribution) 4 3 2 1 0 s rs ic der de ubl a ol p le eh al ct k r oje st a ne pr ge Group provision of medicinal plants improvement of neighborhood attachment improvement of local microclimate Urban agriculture is the practice of growing plant- or animal-based products in urban settings such as community gardens and rooftop farms. Esther Sany é-Mengual, Kathrin Specht, and their team surveyed three groups of people in Bologna, Italy—leaders of urban agriculture projects, stakeholders in urban agriculture (e.g., food researchers and urban farming associations), and the general public — to compare their views about the extent to which urban agriculture contributes to 25 social or ecological services that the team identified. The survey results show that, on average, project leaders rated urban agriculture as contributing less to ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"chart","note":"[Graph: Average Ratings of Urban Agriculture’s Contribution to Various Social or Ecological Services — not available here]"},{"type":"text","text":"Urban agriculture is the practice of growing plant- or animal-based products in urban settings such as community gardens and rooftop farms. Esther Sany é-Mengual, Kathrin Specht, and their team surveyed three groups of people in Bologna, Italy—leaders of urban agriculture projects, stakeholders in urban agriculture (e.g., food researchers and urban farming associations), and the general public — to compare their views about the extent to which urban agriculture contributes to 25 social or ecological services that the team identified. The survey results show that, on average, project leaders rated urban agriculture as contributing less to ______"}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the assertion?","choices":["providing medicinal plants than to improving the local microclimate.","improving community members sense of attachment to the neighborhood than to offering educational opportunities.","improving the local microclimate than to providing medicinal plants.","improving the local microclimate than to providing tourist attractions."],"unusable":"missing graph"},{"id":"44da37eb","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"The variety of species with adaptations to produce toxins is matched by the variety of uses of those toxins: northern stargazers, for example, use toxins for defense, whereas tiger snakes use toxins for predation and skeleton shrimp use toxins for intraspecific competition. In fact, a species may have adaptations enabling it to produce a toxin with multiple uses. Finding that the venom used by the Panamanian scorpion Centruroides granosus to subdue prey also inhibits growth of the pathogenic bacteria Escherichia coli, Dumas Gálvez and colleagues conclude that the particular form of venom production observed in C. granosus may have propagated through the species because it mitigates risk during feeding in addition to enhancing predation success.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The variety of species with adaptations to produce toxins is matched by the variety of uses of those toxins: northern stargazers, for example, use","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"toxins for defense, whereas tiger snakes use toxins for predation and skeleton shrimp use toxins for intraspecific competition. In fact, a species","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"may have adaptations enabling it to produce a toxin with multiple uses. Finding that the venom used by the Panamanian scorpion Centruroides","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"granosus to subdue prey also inhibits growth of the pathogenic bacteria Escherichia coli, Dumas Gálvez and colleagues conclude that the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"particular form of venom production observed in C. granosus may have propagated through the species because it mitigates risk during feeding","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"in addition to enhancing predation success.","indent":0}],"question":"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Gálvez and colleagues’ conclusion?","choices":["E. coli does not appear to be virulent for C. granosus even when transmitted from prey captured without the use of venom.","E. coli is frequently found in species preyed on by C. granosus and can survive exposure to the digestive juices of C. granosus.","C. granosus appears to be chemically sensitive to prey infected with E. coli and tends to favor uninfected individuals.","Exposure to C. granosus venom also inhibits the growth of nonpathogenic bacteria species common in the native environment of C. granosus."]},{"id":"923ebfe3","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"Businesses selling clothing and other fashion items face obstacles in trying to forecast how much product to order: tastes and styles change quickly, while manufacturing clothing takes a significant amount of time. Researchers Youran Fu and Marshall Fisher have found that combining sellers own data with information gathered from social media can dramatically improve the accuracy of such forecasts — by 24 to 57 percent in the cases they directly studied. Better predictions mean demand is easier to meet without retailers becoming overstocked.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Businesses selling clothing and other fashion items face obstacles in trying to forecast how much product to order: tastes and styles change","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"quickly, while manufacturing clothing takes a significant amount of time. Researchers Youran Fu and Marshall Fisher have found that combining","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"sellers’ own data with information gathered from social media can dramatically improve the accuracy of such forecasts—by 24 to 57 percent in","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the cases they directly studied. Better predictions mean demand is easier to meet without retailers becoming overstocked.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Using multiple data sources can enhance the ability of sellers in the fashion industry to anticipate demand.","Social media is revolutionizing how both sellers and researchers view the fashion industry.","Becoming overstocked is the main preoccupation of sellers trying to forecast demand for fashion items.","Retailers can use their own data to accurately predict how tastes and styles are evolving."]},{"id":"ede3f942","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Total Areas of Five Tribal Nations in California Tribal nation Location Area (square miles) Hoopa Valley Tribe Northern California 141.68 La Jolla Band of Luise ño Indians Southern California 13.50 Pauma Band of Luise ño Mission Indians Southern California 9.36 Agua Caliente Band of Cahuilla Indians Northern California 53.68 Los Coyotes Band of Cahuilla and Cupe ño Indians Southern California 39.21 In what is now the state of California, there are over 100 tribal nations. One of the largest, the Hoopa Valley Tribe, covers 141.68 square miles in the northern part of the state. However, most tribal nations in California are less than 50 square miles in total area. For example, the total area of the Los Coyotes Band of Cahuilla and Cupe ño Indians in Southern California is ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"table","title":"Total Areas of Five Tribal Nations in California","headers":["Tribal nation","Location","Area (square miles)"],"rows":[["Hoopa Valley Tribe","Northern California","141.68"],["La Jolla Band of Luiseño Indians","Southern California","13.50"],["Pauma Band of Luiseño Mission Indians","Southern California","9.36"],["Agua Caliente Band of Cahuilla Indians","Northern California","53.68"],["Los Coyotes Band of Cahuilla and Cupeño Indians","Southern California","39.21"]]},{"type":"text","text":"In what is now the state of California, there are over 100 tribal nations. One of the largest, the Hoopa Valley Tribe, covers 141.68 square miles in the northern part of the state. However, most tribal nations in California are less than 50 square miles in total area. For example, the total area of the Los Coyotes Band of Cahuilla and Cupe ño Indians in Southern California is ______"}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the statement?","choices":["13.50 square miles.","53.68 square miles.","9.36 square miles.","39.21 square miles."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["Total Areas of Five Tribal","Nations in California"]},{"id":"c188a397","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"Recently, scientists looked at data collected by NASA’s InSight lander to learn more about seismic activity on Mars, known as marsquakes. The data show that the marsquakes all started from the same location on the planet. This discovery was surprising to scientists, as they expected that the marsquakes would originate from all over the planet because of the cooling of the planet’s surface. Now, scientists believe that there could be areas of active magma flows deep beneath the planet’s surface that trigger the marsquakes.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Recently, scientists looked at data collected by NASA’s InSight lander to learn more about seismic activity on Mars, known as marsquakes. The","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"data show that the marsquakes all started from the same location on the planet. This discovery was surprising to scientists, as they expected","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"that the marsquakes would originate from all over the planet because of the cooling of the planet’s surface. Now, scientists believe that there","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"could be areas of active magma flows deep beneath the planet’s surface that trigger the marsquakes.","indent":0}],"question":"According to the text, what was surprising to scientists studying the seismic activity data from NASA’s InSight lander?","choices":["The surface temperature of Mars has been rising.","There were different types of seismic waves causing marsquakes.","NASA’s InSight lander collected less data than scientists had expected.","All the marsquakes started from the same location on the planet."]},{"id":"95146ebb","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"The ice melted on a Norwegian mountain during a particularly warm summer in 2019, revealing a 1,700-year-old sandal to a mountaineer looking for artifacts. The sandal would normally have degraded quickly, but it was instead well preserved for centuries by the surrounding ice. According to archaeologist Espen Finstad and his team, the sandal, like those worn by imperial Romans, wouldn’t have offered any protection from the cold in the mountains, so some kind of insulation, like fabric or animal skin, would have needed to be worn on the feet with the sandal.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The ice melted on a Norwegian mountain during a particularly warm summer in 2019, revealing a 1,700-year-old sandal to a mountaineer looking","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"for artifacts. The sandal would normally have degraded quickly, but it was instead well preserved for centuries by the surrounding ice. According","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"to archaeologist Espen Finstad and his team, the sandal, like those worn by imperial Romans, wouldn’t have offered any protection from the cold","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"in the mountains, so some kind of insulation, like fabric or animal skin, would have needed to be worn on the feet with the sandal.","indent":0}],"question":"What does the text indicate about the discovery of the sandal?","choices":["Temperatures contributed to both protecting and revealing the sandal.","The discovery revealed that the Roman Empire had more influence on Norway than archaeologists previously assumed.","Archaeologists would have found the sandal eventually without help from the general public.","The sandal would have degraded if it hadn’t been removed from the ice."]},{"id":"29c9be28","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"To survive when water is scarce, embryos inside African turquoise killifish eggs ______ a dormant state known as diapause. In this state, embryonic development is paused for as long as two years — longer than the life span of an adult killifish.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"To survive when water is scarce, embryos inside African turquoise killifish eggs ______ a dormant state known as diapause. In this state,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"embryonic development is paused for as long as two years—longer than the life span of an adult killifish.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["enter","to enter","having entered","entering"]},{"id":"f40ca576","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Each year in the fall, when the weather starts to cool in the northern hemisphere, millions of North American monarch butterflies journey south. Searching for food and warmer habitats, they ______ thousands of miles — from as far north as Canada all the way down to Mexico —on this annual migration.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Each year in the fall, when the weather starts to cool in the northern hemisphere, millions of North American monarch butterflies journey south.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Searching for food and warmer habitats, they ______ thousands of miles—from as far north as Canada all the way down to Mexico—on this","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"annual migration.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["flew","were flying","had flown","fly"]},{"id":"ba8ebf49","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"The poem Beowulf begins with the word hwæt, which is an Old English ______ as hark! or listen! in some versions, the word was playfully rendered as “bro!” by Maria Dahvana Headley in her 2020 translation of the poem.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The poem Beowulf begins with the word “hwæt,” which is an Old English ______ as “hark!” or “listen!” in some versions, the word was playfully","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"rendered as “bro!” by Maria Dahvana Headley in her 2020 translation of the poem.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["exclamation, translated","exclamation and translated","exclamation translated","exclamation. Translated"]},{"id":"a1e0c981","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"In her book The Woman Warrior: Memoirs of a Girlhood Among Ghosts, author Maxine Hong Kingston examines themes ______ childhood, womanhood, and Chinese American identity by intertwining autobiography and mythology.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In her book The Woman Warrior: Memoirs of a Girlhood Among Ghosts, author Maxine Hong Kingston examines themes ______ childhood,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"womanhood, and Chinese American identity by intertwining autobiography and mythology.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["of:","of","of —","of,"]},{"id":"b0115ef6","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Technologies such as microphones and inkjet printers are made using piezoelectric materials, which generate an internal electric field when pressure is applied to them. The toxic nature of some of these materials recently led a team from the University of Sheffield to investigate how ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Technologies such as microphones and inkjet printers are made using piezoelectric materials, which generate an internal electric field when","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"pressure is applied to them. The toxic nature of some of these materials recently led a team from the University of Sheffield to investigate how","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["could their use be better regulated?","their use could be better regulated.","their use could be better regulated?","could their use be better regulated."]},{"id":"cd2443c0","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"A pioneer in the field of taphonomy (the study of how organic remains become fossils), ______ may be just as prevalent in the fossil record as those of thick-shelled organisms.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"A pioneer in the field of taphonomy (the study of how organic remains become fossils), ______ may be just as prevalent in the fossil record as","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"those of thick-shelled organisms.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["fossils of thin-shelled organisms, Anna Behrensmeyer demonstrated in a 2005 analysis,","Anna Behrensmeyer demonstrated in a 2005 analysis that fossils of thin-shelled organisms","it was demonstrated in a 2005 analysis by Anna Behrensmeyer that fossils of thin-shelled organisms","a 2005 analysis — by Anna Behrensmeyer —demonstrated that fossils of thin-shelled organisms"]},{"id":"1b219d14","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"As a young historian in the 1950s, Alixa Naff began interviewing fellow Arab American immigrants about their experiences straddling two cultures. Over the next few decades, Naff conducted more than 450 such interviews, also known as oral histories. ______ she collected photographs and other artifacts that represented her subjects’ experiences.","question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["In other words,","On the contrary,","In addition,","Today,"]},{"id":"c78620ba","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"In 1968, US Congressman John Conyers introduced a bill to establish a national holiday in honor of Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. The bill didn’t make it to a vote, but Conyers was determined. He teamed up with Shirley Chisholm, the first Black woman to be elected to Congress, and they resubmitted the bill every session for the next fifteen years. ______ in 1983, the bill passed.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In 1968, US Congressman John Conyers introduced a bill to establish a national holiday in honor of Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. The bill didn’t make","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"it to a vote, but Conyers was determined. He teamed up with Shirley Chisholm, the first Black woman to be elected to Congress, and they","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"resubmitted the bill every session for the next fifteen years. ______ in 1983, the bill passed.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Instead,","Likewise,","Finally,","Additionally,"]},{"id":"a40c7aa3","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"Most of the planets that have been discovered outside our solar system orbit G-type stars, like our Sun. In 2014, ______ researchers identified a planet orbiting KELT-9, a B-type star more than twice as massive and nearly twice as hot as the Sun. Called KELT-9b, it is one of the hottest planets ever discovered.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Most of the planets that have been discovered outside our solar system orbit G-type stars, like our Sun. In 2014, ______ researchers identified a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"planet orbiting KELT-9, a B-type star more than twice as massive and nearly twice as hot as the Sun. Called KELT-9b, it is one of the hottest","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"planets ever discovered.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["likewise,","however,","therefore,","for example,"]},{"id":"4105f5ac","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"When, in 2017, Cambridge University students Lucy Moss and Toby Marlow decided they wanted to develop a musical together, one of their goals was for their female actor friends to have good parts to play. ______ they created the show Six, a retelling of the history of King Henry VIII’s wives in which each of the six queens has a starring role.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"When, in 2017, Cambridge University students Lucy Moss and Toby Marlow decided they wanted to develop a musical together, one of their goals","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"was for their female actor friends to have good parts to play. ______ they created the show Six, a retelling of the history of King Henry VIII’s wives","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"in which each of the six queens has a starring role.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["In other words,","In summary,","For example,","To that end,"]},{"id":"dede8260","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: When medical students mention their patients on social media, they may violate patient confidentiality. Terry Kind led a study to determine how many medical schools have student policies that mention social media use. Kind and her team reviewed 132 medical school websites, examining publicly available student policies. Only thirteen medical schools had guidelines that explicitly mention social media, and only five defined what constitutes acceptable social media use. The student wants to emphasize the study’s methodology.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"When medical students mention their patients on social media, they may violate patient confidentiality.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Terry Kind led a study to determine how many medical schools have student policies that mention social media use.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Kind and her team reviewed 132 medical school websites, examining publicly available student policies.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Only thirteen medical schools had guidelines that explicitly mention social media, and only five defined what constitutes acceptable","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"social media use.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The student policies of 132 medical schools can be found online, according to research by Terry Kind.","To find out how many medical schools have guidelines about student social media use, Terry Kind and her team examined the student policies of 132 medical schools.","Out of 132 medical schools, only thirteen had student policies that mentioned social media, and only five specified what use was acceptable.","Terry Kind and her team wanted to know how many medical schools have student social media policies in place about protecting patient confidentiality."]},{"id":"e1b00a70","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"The more diverse and wide ranging an animal’s behaviors, the larger and more energy demanding the animal’s brain tends to be. ______ from an evolutionary perspective, animals that perform only basic actions should allocate fewer resources to growing and maintaining brain tissue. The specialized subtypes of ants within colonies provide an opportunity to explore this hypothesis.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The more diverse and wide ranging an animal’s behaviors, the larger and more energy demanding the animal’s brain tends to be. ______ from an","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"evolutionary perspective, animals that perform only basic actions should allocate fewer resources to growing and maintaining brain tissue. The","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"specialized subtypes of ants within colonies provide an opportunity to explore this hypothesis.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Subsequently,","Besides,","Nevertheless,","Thus,"]}];
